<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Media Atropatene</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Media Atropatene was a kingdom in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, roughly corresponding to present-day Iranian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. It was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atropates">Atropates</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s conquest and remained largely independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. Its name is the origin of the regional name Azerbaijan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/media-atropatene.jpg" alt="Media Atropatene" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Golden · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atropatene_as_a_vassal_of_Seleucids.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Atropatene, also called Atropatia or Atropatian Media, was an ancient Iranian kingdom, established about 323 BCE by the Persian satrap <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atropates">Atropates</a> and centred mostly on the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> name, Ātṛpātakāna, is the direct ancestor of the name of the historic Azerbaijan region. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Media was divided into two parts: Greater Media, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a> as its metropolis, and Atropatian Media, which took its name from the commander Atropates, who prevented this part from becoming subject to the Macedonians. Medieval Arab geographers instead derived the name from Adorbador, a priest whose name means guardian of the fire. Its main hub was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganzak">Ganzak</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-urmia">Lake Urmia</a>, which presumably served as the capital of Atropates and his successors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atropates">Atropates</a> fought alongside the Achaemenid army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Battle of Gaugamela</a> in 331 BCE, then declared loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, who appointed him governor of Media in 328 to 327 BCE. After Alexander's death in 323 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> was divided: Media Magna went to Peithon, one of Alexander's bodyguards, and the smaller northern region became Media Atropatene under Atropates, who had by then become father-in-law of the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a>. It became an independent kingdom. In 223 BCE Antiochus III of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> attacked it and won, and its king Artabazanes accepted Seleucid ascendancy while keeping internal independence.</p><p>Later, Atropatene allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 36 BCE the Roman general Antony besieged the city of Fraaspa and withdrew after a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, losing about thirty-five thousand soldiers. The dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atropates">Atropates</a> founded ruled for several centuries, first independently and then as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Arsacids, until it was supplanted by a line of the Arsacids in the early 1st century CE. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharasmanes-ii-of-iberia">Pharasmanes II of Iberia</a> devastated the region in 134 to 135 CE, the people of Atropatene allied themselves with the Persian Sasanian prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> against the Parthian Artabanus IV. In 226 the region submitted with little resistance to Ardashir I and became a province governed by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marzban">marzban</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Atropatene was the only Iranian region to remain under Zoroastrian authority from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> to the Arab conquest without interruption, apart from Alexander's brief rule. Under the Atropatids the region gained a dominant place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, which continued into the Sasanian period, and it served as a stronghold of Iranian culture. Local claims also placed the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a> in Atropatene rather than in the east.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Atropatene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
